<!-- FIXTURE
proves: nothing about the scanner. This file documents the directory and is
deliberately clean, which pins the runner's other end: a fixture declaring
`expect: none` is still parsed, scanned and reported.

Note it carries no literal HTML-comment terminator in its prose. The header
parser stops at the first one, so a fixture that quotes the delimiter
inside its own header truncates the parse and loses its expectations —
measured while writing this file.

WHY THE `.md.fixture` EXTENSION, NOT `.md`. These files carry doubled words
on purpose. `checks/markdown-scan.nix` scans every tracked `.md` in the
tree — `checks/fixtures/kiro-steering/alpha.md` and six of its neighbours
are in that corpus today, measured — so a `.md` fixture here would fail the
very check it exists to test. Prettier would also reflow it and destroy the
exact indentation these fixtures encode. Both problems disappear if the
files simply are not `.md`: `find -name '*.md'` does not match them, and no
treefmt formatter claims `.fixture`. The alternative was three new
exclusion entries — the scan's `find`, treefmt's globs, and whatever comes
next — that a future reader has to keep in step; this way there is nothing
to keep in step.

HOW TO ADD ONE. Open with a FIXTURE header comment stating what the fixture
proves, then one `expect:` line per hit, `expect: <line> "<bigram>"`, or
`expect: none`. Line numbers are 1-based and count the header, so re-check
them after editing anything above the hit. Every fixture that expects NO
hit from some construct must also carry a control doubling in plain prose
that IS expected — otherwise the fixture passes just as happily when the
scanner never reads the file.
expect: none
-->

# doubled-words fixtures

Run these with `checks/doubled-words-fixtures.nix`, which feeds this
directory to `doubled_words_fixtures.py` in the shared scanner store
directory. By hand, from a checkout:

    python3 checks/doubled-words-fixtures.py checks/fixtures/doubled-words
